<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rupert of Germany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1352">1352</a>–<a href="/years/1410">1410</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Rupert of the Palatinate, Ruprecht III · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rupert of the Palatinate was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> and King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> from 1400, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> deposed Wenceslaus, until his death in 1410. His authority was limited, and an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> failed. His sons divided the Palatinate lands, and the marriage of his son Stephen to Anna of Veldenz founded the house of Palatinate-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rupert-of-germany.jpg" alt="Rupert of Germany" width="320" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diözese Speyer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rupprecht_III._von_der_Pfalz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rupert of the Palatinate was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amberg">Amberg</a> in the Upper Palatinate on 5 May 1352, the son of Elector Palatine Rupert II and Beatrice of Aragon, daughter of King Peter II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. He belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a>, and his great-granduncle was the Holy Roman Emperor Louis IV. He was raised at the Dominican Liebenau monastery near Worms, where his widowed grandmother Irmengard of Oettingen lived as a nun. He took part in governing the Electoral Palatinate from his early years and succeeded his father in 1398. In 1374 he married Elisabeth, daughter of Burgrave Frederick V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 20 August 1400 Rupert and the three ecclesiastical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> met at Lahneck Castle and declared King Wenceslaus deposed. The next day the same four met at Rhens and chose Rupert as King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Aachen refused to let him in, so Archbishop Frederick III of Saarwerden crowned him at Cologne on 6 January 1401.</p><p>Lacking a solid power base, he was contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-luxembourg">House of Luxembourg</a>, Wenceslaus's family. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a> he backed the Roman pope Boniface IX, who was reluctant to acknowledge him. He crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in autumn 1401 to break the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and to be crowned emperor, but was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in April 1402.</p><p>He won the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> when his son Louis married Blanche of Lancaster, daughter of Henry IV, in July 1402, and in his Palatinate lands he proved a capable ruler. In 1405 Archbishop Johan II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> formed an alliance with Count Eberhard III of Württemberg, Margrave Bernard I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and several Swabian cities, and Rupert had to make concessions. Only the indolence of Wenceslaus prevented his overthrow.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rupert died on 18 May 1410 at his castle of Landskrone near Oppenheim and was buried in the Church of the Holy Spirit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>. He had divided his lands among his four surviving sons. Louis III became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>, John received the County Palatine of Neumarkt, Stephen that of Simmern-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a> and Otto that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosbach">Mosbach</a>. His efforts earned him the surname Clemens, the Gentle, and he commissioned the Ruprecht building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-castle">Heidelberg Castle</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rupert, King of the Romans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Elector <b>Rupert III</b> was himself elected King of the Romans in 1400, after the deposition of Wenceslaus, and reigned until 1410.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Frederick III died in 1444 without sons; his daughter Anna had married Stephen, son of <b>King Rupert</b> of the Palatinate.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-veldenz">County of Veldenz</a></li><li><blockquote>George died in 1503 without sons, leaving his lands to his son-in-law <b>Rupert of the Palatinate</b> in breach of Wittelsbach house law.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-landshut">Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the mayor Heinrich Toppler, who died in prison in 1408 after the city fell foul of <b>King Rupert</b>, the city built up one of the largest rural territories of any imperial city.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-rothenburg">Free Imperial City of Rothenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>King Rupert</b>, Elector Palatine, died in 1410, his lands were divided among his four sons.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-mosbach">Palatinate-Mosbach</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cologne arbitration of 1505 that ended the War of the Landshut Succession created a new principality from parts of the Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut for Otto Henry and Philip, the young sons of <b>Rupert of the Palatinate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amberg">Amberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Castle Landskron, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.44,11.85&amp;year=1389" aria-label="Amberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Rupert of Germany"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5654 5839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5654 5839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.44,11.85&amp;year=1389">What was here in 1389? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
